#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059392 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3332b534-bd04-5e86-995e-1e6d691d8e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "762e323d-62be-5cd0-ad06-7b11af56fe3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3136" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\sin^2\\theta$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta})\n$$\n\nThen, we use\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\n$$\n\nto simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} \\theta  \\cdot (\\sin^{2} \\theta  + \\cos^{2}) &= \\sin^{2} \\theta  \\cdot 1 = \\sin^{2} \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2\\theta\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059393 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "868125f0-22e0-57fc-9620-f3242419a689" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Rational Expression Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "f2b7e482-2bcd-5bd2-91ee-66415b375da4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3137" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta}{\\sin\\theta}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\cdot (\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta)} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{\\cos   \\theta  \\cdot  ( {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  )}{\\sin   \\theta} & = \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  \\cdot  ( 1 )}{\\sin   \\theta} \\\\  & = \\frac{\\cos   \\theta}{\\sin   \\theta} \\\\  & = \\cot   \\theta\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\cot \\theta\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059394 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bc8c875-3393-5fd6-90cf-87737e16f2f2" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Trigonometric Expression Using a Common Denominator and the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "9ee1e04e-8a36-5597-8928-c2ebd67c1b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3139" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity\n\n$$\n\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{\\sin   \\theta \\cdot  \\sin   \\theta}{\\sin   \\theta  ( 1 + \\cos   \\theta )} + \\frac{\\cos   \\theta \\cdot  ( 1 + \\cos   \\theta  )}{\\sin   \\theta ( 1 + \\cos   \\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sin   \\theta \\cdot  \\sin   \\theta + \\cos   \\theta \\cdot  ( 1 + \\cos   \\theta )}{\\sin   \\theta ( 1 + \\cos   \\theta )} & \\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}  \\theta + \\cos   \\theta + {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta}{\\sin   \\theta ( 1 + \\cos   \\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{( {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta + {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta ) + \\cos   \\theta}{\\sin   \\theta ( 1 + \\cos   \\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1 + \\cos   \\theta}{\\sin   \\theta ( 1 + \\cos   \\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sin   \\theta} & = \\\\ \\csc   \\theta\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec62fd08-5729-5dc4-97b9-1a422f70109e" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "d917e025-59a4-5df5-b412-4eb6f2c4afc7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3329" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - \\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms.\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x -(\\sin^2x + \\cos^2 x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use\n\n$$\n\\sin^2{x} + \\cos^2{x} = 1\n$$\n\nto simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$"}]}
